  "summary": "The cited passages present resurrection as God’s act and as a hope for embodied life. Daniel 12 describes the dead awakening; John 11 records Jesus identifying himself as resurrection and life; John 20 narrates the empty tomb and appearances. Paul proclaims Jesus’ resurrection in Acts and explains in 1 Corinthians 15 how the dead are raised with transformed, imperishable life. First Thessalonians and Philippians address the hope of believers, while Revelation places resurrection within judgment and renewed creation. The texts share hope beyond death but use different genres and images.",
  "historical_context": "The references include Daniel’s vision, Gospel narratives, apostolic preaching in Acts, letters to early Christian communities, and Revelation’s visions. These settings are described from the texts themselves; the source list does not support general claims about how all ancient people understood death.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "",
  "hebraic_worldview": "",
  "second_temple_context": "",
  "canonical_context": "Daniel’s vision gives an early biblical picture of awakening from death. The Gospels narrate Jesus’ resurrection, and Acts proclaims it as central to the apostolic message. Paul connects Christ’s resurrection with the future raising of believers; Revelation places that hope alongside final judgment and new creation. The selected texts connect these claims without making every passage answer the same question.",
  "later_christian_reception": "",
